Detailed Description\par
\pard\plain 
{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Keyboard key tokens. \par
}{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
See key input for how these are used.\par
These key codes are inspired by the {\i USB HID Usage Tables v1.12}  (p. 53-60), but re-arranged to map to 7-bit ASCII for printable keys (function keys are put in the 256+ range).\par
The naming of the key codes follow these rules:{
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
The US keyboard layout is used\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
Names of printable alphanumeric characters are used (e.g. "A", "R", "3", etc.)\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
For non-alphanumeric characters, Unicode:ish names are used (e.g. "COMMA", "LEFT_SQUARE_BRACKET", etc.). Note that some names do not correspond to the Unicode standard (usually for brevity)\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
Keys that lack a clear US mapping are named "WORLD_x"\par
\pard\plain {\*\hyphen2\hyphlead2\hyphtrail2\hyphmax0}\nowidctlpar\cf0\hich\af0\langfe2052\dbch\af0\afs24\lang1081\loch\f0\fs24\lang1033{\listtext\pard\plain \hich\af3\dbch\af3\loch\f8 \'01\u8226\tab}\ilvl0\ls1 \li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi-360\tx360\li360\ri0\lin360\rin0\fi-360\kerning1\hich\af4\dbch\af5\rtlch \ltrch\loch\fs20
For non-printable keys, custom names are used (e.g. "F4", "BACKSPACE", etc.) \par}
\par
